# QUA in this repository

This repo contains **QUA programs embedded in Python** for superconducting qubit control and calibration on Quantum Machines hardware (OPX).

## Official documentation

When writing, reviewing, or debugging QUA code, consult the official docs:

**https://docs.quantum-machines.co/latest/**

Useful entry points:
- [QUA Overview](https://docs.quantum-machines.co/latest/introduction/qua_overview/) — language concepts
- [QUA Language Features](https://docs.quantum-machines.co/latest/guides/qua_language_features/) — syntax and constructs
- [QUA Best Practice Guide](https://docs.quantum-machines.co/latest/guides/qua_best_practice_guide/)
- [Configuration](https://docs.quantum-machines.co/latest/introduction/configuration/) — machine config schema
- [QUA API](https://docs.quantum-machines.co/latest/api_references/qua_language_api/qua_api/) — `declare`, `play`, `measure`, `align`, streams, etc.
- [Quantum Machine API](https://docs.quantum-machines.co/latest/api_references/opx1000/quantum_machine_api/) — compile, execute, simulate jobs

Prefer these docs over general Python or generic quantum-computing assumptions when unsure about QUA syntax, timing, streams, or hardware configuration.

## Python vs QUA

- **Python** (outside `with program()`): setup, parameters, plotting, data handling, `QuantumMachinesManager`, simulation config.
- **QUA** (inside `with program()`): real-time pulse sequences — `declare`, `play`, `measure`, `wait`, `align`, `for_`, `if_`, `declare_output_stream`, `declare_with_stream`, iterables, etc.

Do not rewrite QUA constructs as plain Python loops or NumPy when they must run on the OPX in real time.

## Repo layout

| Path | Role |
|------|------|
| `Quantum-Control-Applications/` | Standalone example/calibration scripts |
| `qualibration_graphs/superconducting/` | QUAlibrate calibration nodes (`create_qua_program`, `execute_qua_program`) |
| `Tutorials/` | Introductory QUAlibrate tutorial |

## Related ecosystem (not QUA itself)

These complement QUA but have their own docs:
- [py-qua-tools](https://github.com/qua-platform/py-qua-tools) — helpers used heavily in scripts (`qualang_tools`)
- [QUAM](https://qua-platform.github.io/quam/) / [quam-builder](https://github.com/qua-platform/quam-builder) — machine abstraction in calibration graphs
- [QUAlibrate](https://qua-platform.github.io/qualibrate/) — calibration orchestration

## Conventions in this repo

- `from qm.qua import *` is standard here; do not flag it as a lint issue.
- Match existing patterns in the nearest folder (standalone script vs qualibration node).
- Format/lint: `uv run black .` and `uv run ruff check .` per [CONTRIBUTING.md](CONTRIBUTING.md).

## QUA 1.3.1 migration

When updating QUA programs for **qm-qua 1.3.1**, follow
[docs/plans/qua-1.3.1-migration.md](../../docs/plans/qua-1.3.1-migration.md).

Prefer **iterables + auto-streaming** for sweep experiments:
- [Iterables & Auto-Streaming (1.3.1)](https://docs.quantum-machines.co/1.3.1/docs/Guides/qua_iterables/)
- [qm-qua 1.3.1 release notes](https://docs.quantum-machines.co/1.3.1/docs/Releases/qm_qua_releases/)

Use `declare_output_stream()` instead of deprecated `declare_stream()`.
Use native QUA 1.3.1 iterables and `declare_with_stream` directly in calibration nodes (see migration plan template).
